  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \n\nThe latest mesoanalysis shows instability has waned across much\nof the area, after the passage of multiple MCSs. A band of 1000\nML CAPE was seen on mesoanalysis along a surface boundary near\nand north of I-80. Showers and thunderstorms will continue along\nthis boundary over the next few hours before diminishing. Expect\nmuch of the remainder of the night to be dry as an additional\nMCS drops SE across the Midwest and Lower Ohio Valley region.\nCancelled the Flood Watch for areas east of I-77. While\nany additional rain is expected to be minimal, left the flood\nwatch for the remainder of the Ohio counties with ongoing\nflooding in some locations, and with small streams and creeks\nstill rising due to runoff from recent heavy rainfall. \n\nRemainder of the earlier discussion is below...\nRelatively low confidence forecast continues as predictability \nhorizon is mainly just the next 6 to 12 hours as thunderstorm \ncomplexes push along the front and then dominate the forecast \nfor the next period of 6-12 hours. \n\nA series of thunderstorm complexes overnight and today has\npushed the effective outflow boundary/front relatively far south\nand stabilized much of the forecast area. The rich moisture and\ninstability extends from central Indiana into southern Ohio and\nsouthern West Virginia, and repeated rounds of thunderstorms\nalong the boundary are likely to keep it from lifting back\nnorth tonight and Wednesday.\n\nWith the effective boundary gradually sagging south with each \nMCS, the axis of heaviest rain later tonight into Wednesday will\nlikely setup from northern Indiana SE into southern Ohio and \nmay be outside of our forecast area.\n\nThereafter the WNW flow pattern continues with nearly daily\nthunderstorm chances with the highest probabilities Thursday and\nSunday. Low probabilities of heavy rainfall/flooding and\ndamaging wind gusts will persist in this pattern.",
